  "query_preview": "..\nCurrently there is few to scattered clouds in Western Pima\ncounty as well as Cochise, Graham, and Greenlee counties. Over\nthe next couple of hours, mid to high level clouds from the\nsouthwest will begin to come into Southeast Arizona. Similar to\nyesterday, storms will struggle to form with the mid level\nwarming and still lack of overall lift/forcing. The only\nexception is in the Sky Islands thanks to orographic lifting.\nThe latest CAMs support this as well with very limited activity\nthis afternoon and evening. \n\nThe rest of this weekend into early next week continue to look\nvery limited, with the best chances south and east of Tucson,\nwhere the best instability is. With the above normal moisture,\nlocally heavy rainfall leading to flash flooding continue to be\nthe primary impacts with these storms.\n\nThe low pressure system in the Pacific Northwest will have a few\nshortwaves pass through as the high pressure begins to slowly\ndrift westward, shifting the flow aloft from SW to SE Monday\ninto Tuesday. With this shift, the storm chances begin to\nincrease in coverage across much of Southeastern Arizona in the\nmid to late in the work week. \n\nTemperatures will be within a couple of degrees of normal through \nWednesday, then dropping to below normal levels late next week.",
